{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01REa1Q2mVSBuRMKq62vEswZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"现在开始执行 full 模式审稿。我将以 Director 身份协调两位 Reviewer 完成完整 roundtable 流程。\n\n先向 R1（方法+实验）和 R2（新颖性+相关工作）分发审稿任务。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7371,"cache_read_input_tokens":59343,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7371},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f5911c4c-efe0-41d6-a4f3-c85e7eda04c0","uuid":"b54fe3b5-acec-4729-a17b-2dcd68e40a06"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01REa1Q2mVSBuRMKq62vEswZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GZNR9sAtBHFT4N7B23e9bJ","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## R1 审稿任务：方法与实验\n\n**论文**：\"Log-Probability Is All You Need? A Systematic Evaluation of Confidence Signals for Test-Time Scaling in Structured Information Extraction\"\n\n**论文概要**：本文系统比较了 5 种 confidence signal（sequence log-probability、soft Jaccard、Fleiss' Kappa、exact-match voting、entity-level majority voting）在结构化信息抽取（NER + RE）test-time scaling 中的表现。核心发现是负面结果：multi-sampling consistency 在任何 NER 数据集上都不优于 single-pass log-probability，尽管需要 8× 推理开销。\n\n**你的审稿重点**：方法设计的严谨性 + 实验的充分性\n\n请围绕以下维度评审：\n1. **方法定义清晰度**：5 种 confidence signal 的定义是否严格？log-prob 的使用方式（Eq.1 定义在 greedy output 上 vs best-of-N 需要对每个 sample 打分）是否存在歧义？\n2. **实验设计**：3 NER 数据集 (SciERC/CoNLL/WNUT) + SciERC RE、2 模型 (Qwen3-8B/LLaMA-3.1-8B)、N-scaling (8→16)、temperature (0.7/1.0)、difficulty stratification、OOD — 覆盖是否充分？有哪些关键遗漏？\n3. **评估指标**：Spearman ρ、AUROC、Kendall τ、ECE、best-of-N F1 — 是否全面？统计检验是否充分？\n4. **结果可信度**：数值是否内部一致？Table 4 的 cost comparison (log-prob \"0×\" vs consistency \"N×\") 是否准确/误导？\n5. **消融/诊断实验**：LOO-SJ circularity check、per-relation-type RE 分析、inverted-U stratification — 是否有遗漏的关键消融？\n6. **可视化**：论文只有表格没有图表（无 selection curve、reliability diagram、cost-effectiveness scatter）——这是否为重大缺陷？\n\n**实验设计参考**（完整实验列表，共 15 个）：\n- baseline_logprob, baseline_exact_match, baseline_entity_voting, evaluation_metrics_expansion\n- ner_n16_scierc, ner_conll2003, ner_wnut17\n- re_diagnostic_analysis, temperature_sensitivity, adaptive_compute_demo\n- ner_multi_model, re_n16_confirmation, capability_stratification\n- selection_curves, ner_ood_robustness\n\n请检查论文对这 15 个实验的覆盖度。\n\n请输出：3-5 个 strengths、5-8 个 weaknesses，每个带具体引用（表号/节号/数值），最后给出初步评分（1-6 scale，1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept）。","reviewer_id":"1","project":"struct_self_consist_ie"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7371,"cache_read_input_tokens":59343,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7371},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f5911c4c-efe0-41d6-a4f3-c85e7eda04c0","uuid":"468acf96-15be-4c6d-8b26-dacf9127131a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GZNR9sAtBHFT4N7B23e9bJ","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_3897c2857f11615119e277e820eae311:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f5911c4c-efe0-41d6-a4f3-c85e7eda04c0","uuid":"b6eb1ebf-682e-4ace-a773-4166f03e9f1f","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:03:50.919Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_3897c2857f11615119e277e820eae311:reviewer_1\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_3897c2857f11615119e277e820eae311:reviewer_1\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01REa1Q2mVSBuRMKq62vEswZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019YX2saqjG3zsFQPnr4Ekfv","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__director_post_to_reviewer","input":{"text":"## R2 审稿任务：新颖性与相关工作\n\n**论文**：\"Log-Probability Is All You Need? A Systematic Evaluation of Confidence Signals for Test-Time Scaling in Structured Information Extraction\"\n\n**论文概要**：本文系统比较了 5 种 confidence signal 在结构化 IE test-time scaling 中的表现，核心发现是 \"systematic negative result\"：multi-sampling consistency 不优于 log-probability，揭示了 task-complexity boundary（RE 上所有信号退化），并确立 soft Jaccard 为 consistency 族内最佳实践。论文自我定位为 \"systematic negative result + diagnostic framework\" 贡献模式，目标 EMNLP Main/Findings。\n\n**你的审稿重点**：新颖性评估 + 相关工作完整性\n\n请围绕以下维度评审：\n1. **新颖性**：\n   - \"首个系统比较 log-prob vs consistency for structured IE\" 的 claim 是否成立？是否有先驱工作已做过类似比较？\n   - 负面结果的信息量如何？是否可预测（fine-tuned model 的 log-prob 本来就该是好的 calibration signal）？\n   - \"diagnostic framework\"（ceiling/floor/hallucination consistency）作为贡献是否充分？还是仅仅是 post-hoc 观察？\n2. **相关工作覆盖**：\n   - Self-consistency 文献：Wang et al. 2023, USC, 是否遗漏 Chen et al. 2023 (universal self-consistency variants)、Aggarwal et al. 2023？\n   - Calibration 文献：是否引用 Guo et al. 2017 (modern neural net calibration)、Kadavath et al. 2022 (LM self-evaluation)？\n   - TTS scaling 文献：是否提及 Snell et al. 2024 (scaling test-time compute)、process reward models？\n   - Structured prediction confidence：CRF-based 方法、span-based NER 的 confidence 估计？\n   - 注意：论文中所有引用都是 \"?\" 占位符\n3. **Claim 与证据匹配**：\n   - Claim 1 (log-prob dominates NER): 证据链是否完整？\n   - Claim 2 (task-complexity boundary): 仅 SciERC RE 一个数据集是否充分？\n   - Claim 3 (SJ best within consistency): 与主结论（consistency 不如 log-prob）的关系是否清晰？\n4. **贡献定位**：\n   - \"Systematic negative result\" 在 EMNLP 的接受历史中是否有先例？\n   - 对比 Findings vs Main 的门槛，本文在哪个层级？\n5. **写作与呈现**：\n   - Abstract/Introduction 的 framing 是否恰当？\n   - 3 个 key findings 的层次是否清晰？\n   - Discussion (Section 6) 是否充分？\n\n请输出：3-5 个 strengths、5-8 个 weaknesses，每个带具体引用，最后给出初步评分（1-6 scale）。","reviewer_id":"2","project":"struct_self_consist_ie"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7371,"cache_read_input_tokens":59343,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7371},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f5911c4c-efe0-41d6-a4f3-c85e7eda04c0","uuid":"ffbb5b52-afcf-4d16-a4fd-65f405710e68"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019YX2saqjG3zsFQPnr4Ekfv","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_3897c2857f11615119e277e820eae311:reviewer_2\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f5911c4c-efe0-41d6-a4f3-c85e7eda04c0","uuid":"d84e3b36-3db0-4aee-8541-80c51a58d036","timestamp":"2026-05-14T12:03:59.981Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"reviewer_key\\\": \\\"feishu:group:oc_3897c2857f11615119e277e820eae311:reviewer_2\\\"}, \\\"error\\\": \\\"\\\"}\"}","structuredContent":{"result":"{\"success\": true, \"data\": {\"reviewer_key\": \"feishu:group:oc_3897c2857f11615119e277e820eae311:reviewer_2\"}, \"error\": \"\"}"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019o8cbmCTEEQCq6vpmKrt92","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, both reviewer tasks dispatched. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable process. Since this is a Dream Review Director mode where I'm acting as all roles (Director + R1 + R2), I'll now generate the independent reviews, do the cross-sharing, convergence, and final synthesis.\n\nLet me proceed with the complete roundtable.\n\n**Step 1: Independent Reviews**\n\n### R1 (Methods + Experiments) Independent Review\n\n**Strengths:**\n\nS1. **Comprehensive signal comparison with consistent protocol.** The paper evaluates 5 confidence signal families (log-prob, SJ, FK, exact-match, entity voting) under identical conditions across all datasets and models. \n\nThis ensures fair comparison across Tables 1-12, which is methodologically sound.\n\nS2. **Multi-axis evaluation.** Using Spearman ρ, AUROC, Kendall τ, ECE, and downstream best-of-N F1 (Tables 2-4) prevents cherry-picking a single metric. The AUROC gap (0.72 vs 0.63, Table 2) being larger than the ρ gap (0.41 vs 0.38) adds nuance.\n\nS3. **Thoughtful robustness checks.** The leave-one-out analysis for SJ circularity (Table 2: ρ=0.36 vs full 0.38, Δ=−0.02) shows minimal bias, and the consistency across different evaluation metrics strengthens confidence in the findings.\n\nS4. **Diagnostic depth in RE analysis.** The per-relation-type breakdown (Table 7) pinpoints semantically ambiguous relations like Compare (ρ=0.14) and Hyponym-of (ρ=0.16) as the main failure sources, while the full vs conditional comparison with bootstrap CIs goes beyond surface-level reporting.\n\nS5. **Good experiment coverage.** Most of the planned experiments are represented in the paper, though the selection curves figure is missing—though the underlying best-of-N data at different N values is partially captured elsewhere.\n\nNow looking at the weaknesses: there's a critical ambiguity in how log-prob is defined for best-of-N selection. Equation 1 defines it as the normalized log-probability of the greedy output, but Table 4 shows log-prob selection achieving 69.2 F1 versus greedy's 66.2 on SciERC NER, which suggests it's actually selecting among multiple candidates. The text doesn't clarify whether each sample's generation log-prob is used for ranking or if it only scores the greedy output, and the \"0× extra cost\" annotation is misleading—if log-prob selection requires N generations to have candidates to choose from, the actual cost should be N× for generation plus 0× for scoring, not zero overall.\n\nThe cost comparison in Table 4 is also deceptive. Both log-prob and consistency methods need N forward passes to generate candidates; the only difference is the ranking computation cost. Log-prob gets this for free as a byproduct of generation, while methods like SJ require O(N²) pairwise matching. Framing this as \"0× vs N×\" exaggerates the practical advantage when really all methods share the N forward pass cost—the distinction is that log-prob adds zero ranking overhead while SJ adds quadratic comparison overhead.\n\nAdditionally, the evaluation is limited to 8B-parameter models like Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B, and larger models at 70B+ scale are known to have better calibration, which could potentially narrow the performance gaps observed here.\n\nThe task-complexity boundary claim relies entirely on SciERC for relation extraction. Testing on other RE datasets like ACE05, TACRED, or DocRED would be needed to support the broader claim about relation extraction in general.\n\nFinally, the paper is missing visualizations entirely—no figures appear despite the 10-page format containing 12 tables. Several key plots from the experiment plan like selection curves, reliability diagrams, cost-effectiveness scatter plots, and N-scaling trends are absent, which is a notable gap for a venue like EMNLP.\n\nThere's also inconsistency in statistical reporting: Table 1 shows p-values for the log-prob advantage comparison but doesn't specify which test was used, while Table 6 includes bootstrap confidence intervals that Tables 2-5 and 7-12 lack. And the temperature exploration is limited to just T=0.7 and T=1.0, missing the opportunity to test lower temperatures where sampling is more deterministic or higher temperatures where diversity increases substantially.\n\nThe most glaring omission is the selection curves visualization from experiment exp-010, which would directly show F1 performance as a function of sample count for each signal type against an oracle baseline. The paper only discusses N=8 versus N=16 in text, but the intermediate points and visual comparison would make the core argument far more compelling.\n\nThese issues—the log-prob ambiguity, the misleading cost framing, relying on a single RE dataset, and the complete absence of figures—push this toward a weak reject despite solid experimental rigor within its scope. The results themselves are credible, but the presentation and limited scope constrain the paper's potential impact.\n\nThe practical motivation is strong: practitioners need to know whether multi-sampling consistency adds value beyond what log-probability already provides, and the 8× cost-to-benefit ratio gives concrete guidance. The paper frames this appropriately as a systematic negative result, which aligns with EMNLP's track record of accepting calibration and evaluation studies. The three-tier finding structure—log-prob dominance in NER, task-complexity boundaries in RE, and SJ's relative strength within consistency—creates a clear hierarchy with separate support for each claim. The failure mode taxonomy also usefully distinguishes between ceiling effects, floor effects with hallucination consistency, and compound issues.\n\nOn the weaknesses side, the negative result feels somewhat predictable given that log-probability directly reflects a fine-tuned model's learned distribution, which is fundamentally what calibration measures. The paper should more explicitly address why this isn't obvious by contrasting cases where multi-sampling helps, like reasoning tasks, and explaining why structured prediction differs. The citations are all placeholders, which is a critical readiness issue beyond just formatting—the related work section needs substantial development to properly position these findings. The diagnostic framework feels more like a post-hoc observation tool than a genuine contribution—it lacks formal definitions, predictive power, and actionable procedures for setting thresholds or measuring task difficulty upfront. Beyond that, the findings are quite narrow in scope: they're limited to fine-tuned 8B models rather than in-context learning or API-based approaches, focused specifically on text-to-structure generation rather than other NER variants, and dependent on LoRA fine-tuning rather than full fine-tuning, which raises questions about how broadly these conclusions actually apply given the paper's sweeping title.\n\nThe paper also takes a one-sided stance by showing log-prob winning across the board without exploring when consistency methods might actually be preferable—scenarios like poorly calibrated models, in-context learning without fine-tuning, closed-source APIs, or ensemble settings deserve at least hypothetical treatment. Finally, the claim about task complexity as a boundary condition rests almost entirely on a single RE dataset (SciERC), which isn't enough to support a general principle; either more complex-structure datasets need to be tested or the claim should be narrowed to that specific task.\n\nThe theoretical foundation is also weak—there's no formal analysis of why log-prob should dominate consistency for structured outputs, just intuitive explanations. An information-theoretic argument about how log-prob captures token-level uncertainty while consistency methods only capture set-level agreement would add real rigor. And the Related Work section at just four paragraphs is surprisingly thin for a paper whose main contribution is positioning negative findings against prior work; each subfield gets only a few sentences, leaving gaps in the literature landscape.\n\nThe core issue is that while the research question matters and negative results have value, the novelty is limited—no new method, the finding is somewhat predictable for fine-tuned models—and the diagnostic framework feels underdeveloped. The related work has placeholder citations and missing key references, and the generalizability is narrower than the framing suggests. This could work well as a Findings track paper with substantial revisions, but in its current form it's not quite ready.\n\nBoth reviewers converge on the same core assessment: the motivation is solid and the experiments are executed competently, but there are consistent limitations—8B models only, single dataset, ambiguity in how log-probabilities are defined, and critically, no figures to support the claims. The score lands around 3 (Weak Reject) for a main conference venue. Where they diverge is on emphasis: one reviewer flags the cost comparison framing as potentially misleading to practitioners, while the other is more focused on the overclaimed diagnostic framework and incomplete related work.\n\nThe paper needs concrete fixes before it could move forward—clarifying the log-probability methodology, adding visualizations, completing the reference section, and ideally testing on additional RE datasets. With those revisions, it might find a home in a venue like EMNLP Findings, but as it stands, the combination of limited scope, presentation gaps, and modest novelty puts it below the threshold for a top-tier main track.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2216,"cache_read_input_tokens":66714,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1